ETHIO-TRIP
REQUEST AND RESPONSE ANALYSIS
Request and Response Analysis explains how the Ethio-Trip application exchanges data between the frontend and backend. It describes the information sent by the client, how the server processes that information, and the responses returned to the client.
Ethio-Trip uses API communication to support operations such as user authentication, destination management, trip planning, and other application features.
The communication follows the HTTP request-response model.
 Request and Response Architecture
The application follows a client-server communication process:
1.	The user interacts with the frontend.
2.	The frontend collects the required information.
3.	The frontend sends an HTTP request to the appropriate API endpoint.
4.	The server validates and processes the request.
5.	The server returns an HTTP response.
6.	The frontend handles the response and updates the user interface.
Communication Flow
Client ( Next.js) → HTTP Request → API Route → Server Processing → HTTP Response → Client UI
HTTP Request Structure
An HTTP request contains the following main elements:
Component	Description
Method	Defines the requested operation.
URL	Identifies the API endpoint.
Headers	Provide additional request information.
Body	Contains data sent to the server, when required.
Common HTTP Methods
Method	Purpose
GET	Retrieve information.
POST	Submit information or create a resource.
PUT	Replace an existing resource.
PATCH	Update part of an existing resource.
DELETE	Remove a resource.
 HTTP Response Structure
The server returns a response after processing a request.
A response normally contains:
•	Status Code: Indicates the result of the request.
•	Headers: Provide information about the response.
•	Response Body: Contains returned data or an error message.
Common HTTP Status Codes
Status Code	Meaning
200	OK – Request completed successfully.
201	Created – A resource was created successfully.
400	Bad Request – Invalid request data.
401	Unauthorized – Authentication is required or invalid.
403	Forbidden – The user does not have permission.
404	Not Found – The requested resource does not exist.
500	Internal Server Error – An unexpected server error occurred.
 Authentication Request and Response Analysis
Authentication allows users to access the platform using their registered credentials.
1 User Registration
Endpoint: /api/auth/register
HTTP Method: POST
Purpose: Creates a new customer account.
Request Body:
{
  "email": "customer@example.com",
  "password": "examplePassword123"
}
Successful Response:
{
  "message": "Account created successfully"
}

 Error Response:
{
  "error": "Unable to create account"
}
Analysis:
The frontend collects the user's email and password and sends them to the registration API. The server processes the registration request through the authentication service and returns the result. The frontend displays an appropriate success or error message.
2 User Login
Endpoint: /api/auth/login
HTTP Method: POST
Purpose: Authenticates a customer or guide.
 Request Body:
{
  "email": "customer@example.com",
  "password": "examplePassword123"
}
 Successful Response:
{
  "message": "Login successful"
}
Error Response:
{
  "error": "Invalid email or password"
}
Analysis:
The frontend sends the user's credentials to the login API. The server verifies the credentials and determines whether the user is authorized to access the platform. When authentication succeeds, the application establishes the session using its configured cookie-based session mechanism.
The frontend then directs the user to the appropriate dashboard based on their role.
