 Error Handling Analysis
Error handling ensures that the application responds appropriately when a request fails.
Common situations include:
•	Missing required input fields.
•	Incorrect login credentials.
•	Invalid or expired sessions.
•	Unauthorized access.
•	Unavailable resources.
•	Unexpected server errors.
•	Network connection problems.
The frontend should interpret the response and provide understandable feedback instead of leaving the user without an explanation.
For example:
try {
  const response = await fetch("/api/auth/login", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: {
      "Content-Type": "application/json"
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(formData)
  });

  const data = await response.json();

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(data.error || "Login failed");
  }

  console.log("Login successful", data);

} catch (error) {
  console.error("Request failed:", error.message);
}
This example demonstrates how the frontend sends a request, checks the HTTP response, and handles errors.
 Security Considerations
Ethio-Trip considers the following security principles in request and response communication:
•	Authentication credentials should be transmitted over HTTPS in production.
•	Passwords should never be exposed in API responses.
•	Sensitive server-side environment variables must not be exposed to the browser.
•	Session cookies should use appropriate security settings.
•	Protected resources should require authentication and authorization.
•	Input data should be validated before processing.
•	Error messages should not expose sensitive internal information.
 Performance Considerations
Efficient request and response handling improves application performance.
Ethio-Trip uses or incorporates the following techniques where implemented:
•	SWR for data fetching, caching, and revalidation.
•	Next.js Server Components to reduce unnecessary client-side JavaScript.
•	Appropriate loading and error states.
•	Local storage for selected client-side information.
•	Responsive rendering for different screen sizes.
These techniques help improve the user experience and reduce unnecessary data requests.
 Conclusion
Request and Response Analysis demonstrates how Ethio-Trip handles communication between the frontend and backend services. It explains the role of HTTP methods, API endpoints, request bodies, response bodies, status codes, authentication, and error handling.
Understanding this communication process helps developers maintain, debug, test, and improve the application. It also provides a clear technical explanation of how user interactions are translated into application operations.

